Crushing Blows
The Crushing Blows formula is
(for percentage): ((Attacker weapon Skill - Defender defence skill)*2)-15
Defense from gear does not directly reduce Crushing Blows, though it does help by adding to your avoidance. Crushing Blow chance can only be reduced by having enough avoidance (block/dodge/parry/miss) to push them off the attack table.
Armor
Armor reduces how much Physical damage you take when a player or NPC hits you.
There are four types of Armor that players can equip. From the weakest (least amount of Armor) to the strongest (most amount of Armor):
Cloth - Mage, Priest, Warlock
Leather - Druid, Rogue
Mail - Hunter, Shaman
Plate - Paladin, Warrior
Additionally, Paladins, Shaman and Warrior can equip Shields, an off-hand slot that further increases their Armor.
Although Paladins and Warriors have more innate Armor thanks to their ability to equip Plate Armor and Shields, Druids in Bear Form receive significantly increased value from Armor.
The Armor Cap against level 63 (or Boss) mobs is 17,265. The Armor Cap against level 60 mobs (or players) is 16500.
Block
Block is the ability of a shield to absorb incoming Physical damage, in addition to its Armor rating. When a Paladin, Warrior or Shaman have a Shield equipped, they gain a chance to Block incoming Physical attacks.
Block Rating increases the chance of you blocking an attack, on top of the base amount of 5%.
You're only able to Block an attack when facing your attacker.
Block formula:
5% base + Block Rating + talents + ((Defense skill - enemy's weapon skill) * 0.04)
Block Value
Block Rating and Block Value can be easily confused as they sound similar and affect block, but as you can see on items like The Face of Death The Face of Death they are separate things.
Block Value increases the amount of damage mitigated per blocked attack.
Strength increases your Block Value.
Block Value and Block Rating can be easily mixed up to similar names and purposes.
Dodge
Dodge increases the chance to completely avoid a Physical attack.
Dodge can be gained with Agility for all classes, as well as raw Dodge value in select pieces of gear.
One point of Defense adds 0.04% Dodge.
Weapon Skill reduces the enemy's chance to dodge your attacks by .04% per point.
Night Elves have a innate 1% chance to Dodge thanks to  Quickness.
You're only able to Dodge an attack when facing your attacker.
Here is the formula for calculating your character's dodge percentage:
Base dodge + (Agility / Agility to Dodge ratio) + Talent bonuses + Race Bonuses + Item Bonuses + ((Defense skill - Enemy's attack skill) * 0.04)
